{#-
  Every guest's interface: the virt-install arguments every create in this role
  passes, GPU guests' included, each site through
  `{{ lookup('template', 'guest-interface-args.j2') | trim }}`. They attach the
  guest to libvirt's `default` network with a virtio NIC and add, through
  virt-install's XPath edits of the domain XML:

    <port isolated='yes'/>
    <filterref filter='clean-traffic'>
      <parameter name='CTRL_IP_LEARNING' value='dhcp'/>
      <parameter name='DHCPSERVER' value='192.168.122.1'/>
    </filterref>

  An isolated bridge port forwards only to ports that are not isolated, which
  on `virbr0` is the bridge itself, so a guest reaches DHCP, DNS and the NAT
  uplink but never another guest at layer 2. clean-traffic holds the guest to
  its MAC address and to the address it leased from `default`'s DHCP server, so
  it cannot claim another guest's address by ARP. Host preparation refuses a
  host whose libvirt or virt-install cannot apply either (vm-setup's
  guest-policy.yml).

  A template the sites look up, not a variable: an extra variable, such as a
  pool's extra_vars, overrides any variable, and no pool or seller setting
  places guests on a shared segment.
-#}
--network network=default,model=virtio \
--xml ./devices/interface/port/@isolated=yes \
--xml ./devices/interface/filterref/@filter=clean-traffic \
--xml "./devices/interface/filterref/parameter[@name='CTRL_IP_LEARNING']/@value=dhcp" \
--xml "./devices/interface/filterref/parameter[@name='DHCPSERVER']/@value=192.168.122.1"
